    "explanation": "Demetrius of Phalerum was an Athenian orator and statesman who governed Athens as a sole ruler for about ten years after being appointed by the Macedonian king Cassander. He mattered because he combined practical politics with philosophical learning: a student of Theophrastus and perhaps Aristotle, he was among the first members of the Peripatetic school and used that background to shape policy.\n\nAs governor he introduced important legal reforms but kept an oligarchic, pro‑Cassander regime, which made enemies and led to his exile in 307 BC. He then went first to Thebes and later to the court of Alexandria, where he wrote widely on history, rhetoric, and literary criticism.",
